    "texts":[
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Provenance",
            "textentryhtml":"<p>\n\tMarch 28, 1979, sold by Fine Art of Ancient Lands, New York, to Peter G. Wray (1934-2016), Scottsdale, AZ (no. 876-W); sold to Malcolm Delacorte, Cornwall, NY; sold to Shelby White, New York [1]; 2003, gift of Shelby White to the Princeton University Art Museum.\n</p>\n<p>\n\tNotes:\n\t<br />\n\t[1] Provenance according to correspondence with Peter David Joralemon, dated February 8, 2010\n</p>",
            "remarks":null
        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"Courtly scenes in Maya art often depict lords gazing into mirrors held by diminutive figures, as on the black flask displayed here. These likely represent court sculpture. The exact significance of the scenes is unknown, but other Mesoamerican peoples thought mirrors could be used to conjure ancestors or even foretell future events.\n",
            "remarks":"AAA2_20-T6-2_CLA_FA_9_17_25.pdf - Day 1 installation"
        }
    ],
